{"id":"ko-earnings-q3-2026","title":"KO earnings — Coca-Cola, Q3 2026","section":"Earnings","date":"2026-10-27","html":"<p>Management sees steady growth carried by overseas volume and a raised outlook, and the thing that could spoil it is the shopper at home, who is hunting for value while North America volume sits flat.</p>\n<p>Reported Oct 27, 2026. Next report: not found (one calendar site estimates Feb 8, 2027, unconfirmed). Updated Nov 12, 2026. Research, not investment advice.</p>\n<h3 id=\"the-short-version\">The short version</h3>\n<ul><li><strong>Demand:</strong> steady. Volume grew 5% worldwide (India, China, Mexico, Brazil) and was flat in North America.</li><li><strong>Guidance:</strong> raised. Organic revenue growth at least 5% (was about 5%); comparable EPS growth at least 11% (was 9–10%); free cash flow at least $13.0B (was about $12.4B).</li><li><strong>Biggest worry:</strong> the value-hunting shopper in North America, with restaurants weak and mix eating most of the price increases.</li><li><strong>Chart:</strong> leans up. Price is at a 52-week high; the level that decides it is $91.48 (the old August ceiling area). These are my lines, not yours.</li></ul>\n<h3 id=\"the-numbers\">The numbers</h3>\n<ul><li>Revenue $13.35B against $12.93B expected. A beat.</li><li>GAAP EPS $0.93 against $0.88 (Webull). Comparable EPS $0.91 against $0.87 (Zacks). A beat on both bases; compare like with like, since Webull's &quot;actual&quot; is GAAP and the Zacks pair is adjusted.</li><li>Price/mix was only +1%: about 4 points of pricing, less about 3 points of weaker mix.</li></ul>\n<h3 id=\"what-management-said-about-the-road-ahead\">What management said about the road ahead</h3>\n<ul><li><strong>Rest of 2026:</strong> outlook raised on every line except capital spending, which stays at about $2.2B.</li><li><strong>2027:</strong> a slight currency headwind and an 8% hit to reported revenue from selling bottlers. Full guidance comes with Q4 results.</li><li><strong>Next quarter:</strong> Q4 has six fewer days than a year ago, and logistics costs are flagged.</li><li><strong>Pricing:</strong> value matters in every market and income group, and management expects that to last into 2027. &quot;value continues to be something that cuts across every market and every consumer.&quot; Henrique Braun, CEO.</li><li><strong>Channels:</strong> restaurants are weak; at-home is stronger.</li><li><strong>Costs:</strong> lower input costs helped Europe; higher input costs hurt North America.</li><li><strong>North America:</strong> flat volume against +3% in Q2. Management pointed to July 4 timing falling into Q2 and to supply limits on Topo Chico. Trademark Coca-Cola grew; water, sports drinks, coffee and tea fell.</li></ul>\n<h3 id=\"what-management-is-worried-about\">What management is worried about</h3>\n<ol><li><strong>Affordability and mix.</strong> About 3 points of mix drag against 4 points of pricing; North America volume flat.</li><li><strong>The IRS tax dispute.</strong> About $14B of potential further tax and interest for 2010–2025, plus about $1.4B for the first nine months of 2026 (10-Q). Not in guidance. The appeal was argued June 25, 2026; no ruling found.</li><li><strong>Restaurants and other away-from-home sales.</strong> Called weak; no figure given.</li><li><strong>Costs.</strong> Input costs up in North America; logistics costs flagged for Q4.</li><li><strong>Currency and bottler sales.</strong> An 8% revenue hit in 2027 from refranchising, plus a slight currency headwind.</li></ol>\n<h3 id=\"the-big-worry-the-value-hunting-shopper-at-home-both-ways\">The big worry: the value-hunting shopper at home, both ways</h3>\n<p><strong>What would ease it</strong></p>\n<ul><li>Cheaper fuel. AAA's national average was $3.94 on Nov 11, down from $4.36 a month earlier. October averaged $4.31, the highest October on record (EIA figures, via a Nov 9 article).</li><li>A better mood. University of Michigan sentiment rose to 51.0 in early November from 48.0, and it rose for lower- and middle-wealth households.</li><li>Restaurants stabilising. McDonald's US sales fell 0.2% in Q3 but turned positive in September and October.</li><li>New business. Coca-Cola is to supply KFC's US restaurants from early 2027, replacing PepsiCo (Atlanta Journal-Constitution).</li><li>Supply fixes and a new North America president (Rob Gehring, from Dec 1).</li></ul>\n<p><strong>What would make it worse</strong></p>\n<ul><li>More rate hikes. The Fed desk doc shows December priced for a hike, and the Beige Book says customers are resisting price increases and margins are squeezed.</li><li>Restaurant traffic keeps falling. US fast-food traffic was down 2.6% in Q3 (Revenue Management Solutions, via Nation's Restaurant News).</li><li>Food-benefit limits on soda. 23 states have approval to restrict soda purchases with SNAP benefits; Circana found benefit-funded shopping trips that included soft drinks fell 8 points on the year.</li><li>A rival taking the growth. Keurig Dr Pepper's US soft-drink volume/mix rose 7.2% in Q3 while PepsiCo's North America beverage volume fell 2%. The category is not shrinking evenly.</li><li>Oil turning back up. The energy pages in this project describe Hormuz restrictions that are not yet resolved.</li></ul>\n<p><strong>How this company is placed against it</strong></p>\n<ul><li>It still has pricing: about 4 points taken this quarter, and North America operating income rose 5% on flat volume.</li><li>The growth is overseas, so one weak region does not sink the year. That is why guidance went up anyway.</li><li>Balance sheet is light for a staples company: net debt about 1.3x EBITDA. The IRS case is the one item that could change that picture.</li></ul>\n<h3 id=\"money-plans\">Money plans</h3>\n<ul><li>Capital spending about $2.2B, unchanged.</li><li>Dividend $0.53 a quarter. Buybacks only to offset dilution.</li><li>Selling part of its Africa bottling stake by year-end.</li><li>$6.0B was already paid to the IRS in September 2024 for 2007–2009; the reserve for the rest is $538M.</li></ul>\n<h3 id=\"people-and-governance\">People and governance</h3>\n<ul><li><strong>CEO:</strong> Henrique Braun, since March 31, 2026. Joined the company in 1996; not a founder.</li><li><strong>Previous CEO:</strong> James Quincey moved to Executive Chairman (2026 proxy). Whether he still holds the role this month: not found on a November page.</li><li><strong>CFO:</strong> John Murphy, CFO since 2019 and President since 2022. He has also been interim head of North America since Aug 1.</li><li><strong>Successor named:</strong> not found. A board committee owns CEO succession planning.</li><li><strong>Control:</strong> one class of stock, no controlling holder found. Largest holder is Berkshire Hathaway at 9.29%.</li><li><strong>Chair:</strong> split from the CEO, but the chair is an executive, not independent. Lead independent director: David Weinberg.</li><li><strong>Recent changes:</strong> Jennifer Mann stepped down as North America president on Aug 1 with no reason given; Rob Gehring (most recently at Monster Energy) takes over Dec 1. Investigations: not found.</li><li><strong>How much rests on one person:</strong> little. But note the timing: a new CEO, a vacancy in the weakest region, and the CFO covering three jobs until December.</li></ul>\n<h3 id=\"does-the-chart-agree\">Does the chart agree?</h3>\n<figure class=\"chart\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" data-ticker=\"KO\" viewBox=\"0 0 760 400\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"KO · daily · my levels chart\" font-family=\"Arial, sans-serif\"><text x=\"8\" y=\"18\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"700\" fill=\"#20242C\" class=\"c-ink\">KO · daily · my levels</text><line x1=\"8\" x2=\"610\" y1=\"374.0\" y2=\"374.0\" stroke=\"#E3DACB\" stroke-width=\"1\" class=\"c-grid\"/><line x1=\"8\" x2=\"610\" y1=\"289.0\" y2=\"289.0\" stroke=\"#E3DACB\" stroke-width=\"1\" class=\"c-grid\"/><line x1=\"8\" x2=\"610\" y1=\"204.0\" y2=\"204